  <p>For AWS sign-in use the approved profile and device flow: <code>aws sso login --profile &lt;approved-profile&gt; --use-device-code --no-browser</code>. Give the operator the fresh verification link and code privately, then verify <code>aws sts get-caller-identity --profile &lt;approved-profile&gt;</code> against the expected account and role. Never save the code, tokens or identity output in this public page.</p>
  <p>Transfer roles and recovery methods rather than sharing personal logins. Keep secret locations, exact identifiers, provider configuration, authorised contacts and data-recovery instructions in the restricted operational record. Confirm rotation/revocation with the owners after successful transfer; do not revoke the only working access prematurely.</p>
  <p><strong>Preserve <code>receiptEncryptionKey</code> during Microsoft certificate rotation.</strong> This separate key protects the participant ownership receipts needed for cleanup. Assign its custody, certificate-expiry alerts, renewal and recovery to named owners and deputies. Retain the existing key through ordinary credential renewal and verify that existing receipts remain readable; follow the <a href="/resources/handover/release-and-recovery#onboarding-recovery">onboarding recovery boundaries</a> before any key change.</p>

  <h2 id="ai">Developer AI and scheduled automation</h2>
  <p>AI tools assist research, coding, review, modelling proposals and communications preparation. They are not a dependency for reading the static site. The AWS participant services execute ordinary application code; the checked-in architecture does not establish a public website inference API.</p>
  <p>Contributor tooling includes Codex/Claude arrangements, Ruflo coordination/memory, local source-knowledge connections and approved native provider subscriptions. Availability is host- and session-specific. Configuration in Git is not proof that a tool is registered, reachable or authorised on the successor's machine. Do not transfer credentials through prompts or use a different model transport silently.</p>
  <p>Read <code>AGENTS.md</code> for current execution rules and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for Claude-specific settings. Historical time-bounded subscription notices need checking with the owner. Reusable process recall and repository operational state have different memory stores; neither is a place for participant records, raw mail or credentials.</p>
  <h3>Working-group inbox agent</h3>
  <p>The repository contains a separately scheduled AI inbox workflow, its deterministic policy, prompt, wrapper and systemd definitions. This is operational automation outside the static page runtime. Its tracked manifest freezes a specific model and sequential roles; changing that model or moving the host requires deliberate revalidation. The 26 October configuration preflight passed with nine registered groups, two workspaces permitted for inbox automation, five roles and nine gates; this did not execute a mailbox run.</p>
  <pre><code>{`# Configuration/template preflight only; no mailbox run
node scripts/working-group-inbox-agent.mjs check
node scripts/working-group-inbox-agent.mjs list-groups`}</code></pre>
  <p>The timer definition specifies 10:00 and 17:00 Europe/London on weekdays. <strong>Current activation is unverified here.</strong> Establish which host/scheduler actually owns execution, verify provider/Microsoft authentication, inspect a recent safe outcome and transfer the activation/checkpoint state before enabling a successor schedule. Two active schedulers risk duplicate effects.</p>
  <p>The policy sequence is read, validate, write, read back, reply, checkpoint. Mail and attachments are untrusted evidence. Ambiguous requests stop; the agent must not execute attachments or follow submitted instructions as commands. A configuration preflight proves neither live mailbox permissions nor successful participant handling.</p>

  <h2 id="intake">Teams, SharePoint and the evidence-intake lifecycle</h2>
  <p>The accepted workspace pattern uses live Team membership as participant authority and derived SharePoint access for source material. Organisation folders are isolated: being in a working group does not grant access to another organisation's submissions.</p>
  <p>The nine-group registry contains seven workspaces marked <code>implemented</code> or <code>provisioned</code>: the six property-domain workspaces plus cross-cutting Technology. AWS approval onboarding supports the six property domains. The scheduled inbox agent permits automatic writes only to Finance and Banking and Technology, the two marked implemented. DBT Smart Data and Interoperability remain planned in this registry. These are configuration scopes, not a live tenant census; confirm actual access separately. ADR-0089 also records a separate internal technical subgroup outside this registry; do not assume it is the registered Technology workspace.</p>
  <p>Registration, approval, identity creation, invitations, Team membership, SharePoint access and welcome delivery are different states. The repository includes signup, approval/onboarding, acknowledgement, CRM and Teams-card components. Their templates and default flags do not establish current enablement. Read back the actual service configuration and relevant state before retrying or reporting completion.</p>
  <h3>Participant service flow</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Apply:</strong> the public form validates and records an AWS intake submission. Submission events feed separate consumers; an acknowledgement reports receipt and grants no membership.</li>
    <li><strong>Review:</strong> the CRM bridge projects the application into HubSpot. An authorised human reviews each requested domain independently. Interests, newsletter consent and a CRM contact are not approvals.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply the decision:</strong> the trusted approval integration updates the AWS participant register and effective eligibility. The register owns participant identity bindings and access state; HubSpot is not queried on every sign-in.</li>
    <li><strong>Provision asynchronously:</strong> a durable per-domain operation prepares verified Microsoft membership and organisation-folder access where supported. It reads current decisions before effects, then sends the domain-specific invitation after the required postconditions. Website eligibility does not wait for Microsoft or mail availability.</li>
    <li><strong>Sign in and participate:</strong> Auth0 verifies website identity in the current documented arrangement; the retained Cognito pool serves lifecycle integration. The AWS register and session service enforce entitlement. Neither a provider account nor Microsoft membership independently grants website privileges. Confirm the live provider configuration and comments availability during acceptance.</li>
    <li><strong>Withdraw or recover:</strong> a domain withdrawal removes that domain's approval and initiates the corresponding owned-access cleanup. Remaining approvals and any explicit website allowlist grant are assessed independently. Account/security/privacy restrictions and ambiguous effects require their owning review path; do not replay an invitation to resolve uncertainty.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Website entitlement is an explicit <code>websiteAllowlist</code> grant or a currently approved domain.</strong> The allowlist grants website access without granting a working-group workspace. Withdrawing the last domain leaves website eligibility intact while that allowlist survives; participant lifecycle projections do not override the surviving grant. Inspect both grant sources when investigating access or withdrawal. <code>config/aws/auth-session/identity.mjs</code> owns this predicate.</p>
  <p><code>scripts/website-allowlist.mjs</code> is the operator entry point. Its <code>list</code> command reads current allowlisted accounts; its <code>grant</code> and <code>revoke</code> operations require an explicit reason and write only with <code>--confirm</code>. Use the authorised owner, approved AWS profile and the script's current argument contract. Account details, reasons and output belong in the restricted operational record. Verify effective entitlement and session behaviour after any authorised change.</p>
  <p>The participant-facing <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide">member guide</a> explains the intended journey. The operational owners are <code>config/aws/working-group-interest/</code>, <code>acknowledgement/</code>, <code>hubspot-sync/</code>, <code>hubspot-approval/</code>, <code>auth-session/</code> and <code>src/approval-onboarding/</code>; inspect the owning implementation and service state when troubleshooting.</p>
  <h3>Teams approval cards</h3>
  <p>The Teams bot/card path provides another authorised domain-decision surface and reuses the participant approval boundary. Its owners are <code>config/aws/teams-approvals/</code>, <code>config/aws/teams-approvals-stack.yaml</code> and the corresponding deployment settings in <code>.github/workflows/infra.yml</code>. Transfer bot/app registration, credential renewal, approver-group ownership and notification/decision queue monitoring. Read back the bot identity, installation, connector configuration and <code>ApprovalsEnabled</code> state; the deployment variable is <code>OPDA_TEAMS_APPROVALS_ENABLED</code>.</p>
  <p><strong>ADR-0088's frontmatter remains proposed.</strong> Its dated confirmation records activation on 16 September and, after fixes, a successful signup, Teams approval, invitation, guest redemption and withdrawal exercise on 17 September. Those receipts establish dated operational observations, not current enablement. The proposed frontmatter conflicts with the ADR's statement that enabling the flag constitutes acceptance; reconcile that decision status and current configuration with the owner. The recorded outstanding check is refusal of an authenticated click by someone outside the approver group; the earlier unauthenticated-request refusal does not satisfy it.</p>
  <h3>Evidence intake</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Receive.</strong> Record the source, submitting organisation, date, version, intended use and permitted sharing. An invitation or discussion is not proof that a file was supplied.</li>
    <li><strong>Store and verify.</strong> Use the authorised organisation-isolated workspace; confirm the exact stored artefact and access. Email attachments can be a fallback when organisational controls block tenant access. Never advise bypassing those controls.</li>
    <li><strong>Register and review.</strong> Link the artefact to the existing source/intake record. Keep sensitivity, rights, provenance, review owner, questions and current disposition.</li>
    <li><strong>Model or decline.</strong> Link incorporated concepts to the exact source version and model change. Record exclusions, unresolved questions or reasons for deferral. Upload alone is not incorporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Close the loop.</strong> Acknowledge the actual receipt and communicate the review/model disposition through the authorised channel. Publication requires its own permission and content review.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The inbox policy permits only bounded approved attachment types and verified company identities for automatic organisation-folder intake. Generic-provider identities do not automatically establish a company SharePoint boundary. Failed or partial provisioning must remain visible and must not receive a success claim.</p>
  <p><strong>Outstanding acceptance check:</strong> demonstrate how an approved participant whose employer blocks the OPDA Microsoft tenant can submit evidence and feedback through the agreed alternative. Public reading and an optional sign-in control do not prove that end-to-end journey. Reconcile actual received, stored, reviewed, incorporated and pending material against the private intake records; historical snapshots are not a current submission census.</p>

